    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4cc41ef9_da5f2666",
        "filename": "Documentation/rest-api-verifiers.txt",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 138,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-02-06T12:21:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK, fair enough. Let\u0027s consider all options again.\n\nGoals:\n- allow unsetting properties\n- allow updating a subset of the properties (partial update)\n- allow adding new properties later without breaking existing callers\n\nConstraints:\n- In JSON there is no difference between not setting a field and setting a field to null.\n\n1. Specify explicitly as options which properties should be updated.\n\nThis is what is implemented in this change and what the design doc proposes (at least for updates of verification details).\n\nUpdating a property is done\n- by specifying that the property should be updated by setting an option on the request and\n- by setting a value for the property in the request body\n\nUnsetting a property is done\n- by specifying that the property should be updated by setting an option on the request and\n- by NOT setting a value for the property in the request body\n\nProperties that are not specified as options are not updated and any attempt to set values for them in the request body makes the request fail with 400 Bad Request.\n\nPros:\n- It\u0027s very explicit which properties will be updated by the call.\n- The cases \u0027unsetting a property\u0027 and \u0027not setting a property because it should not be updated\u0027 are clearly distinguished by the presence/absence of the option\n\nCons:\n- more complicated for callers\n- not consistent with existing REST endpoints\n\n2. Define a special value for a property that means that property should be unset.\n\nThis is the approach that we currently use for REST endpoints that support partial updates of property sets. This is the approach that you propose.\n\nUpdating a property is done:\n- by setting a value for the property in the request body\n\nUnsetting a property is done\n- by setting the special value for the property in the request body\n\nProperties that are not set in the request body are not updated.\n\nFor String properties the special value can be the empty string (\"\"), but for properties with other types (e.g. int, enums) this is less clear.\nFor preferences the special value is the default value for the preference, so unsetting a preference is done by setting the default value of this preference (whatever the default is).\n\nPros:\n- consistent with existing REST endpoints\n- intuive for callers?\n\nCons:\n- confusing for callers since for unset they need to know the special value, which may be different per property type or even per property\n\n3. Offer one dedicated REST endpoint per property\n\nPros:\n- very explicit\n- consistent with existing REST endpoints (e.g. we have a dedicated REST endpoint to update the project description)\n\nCons:\n- Atomic updates of multiple properties is not possible.\n- Inefficient (more requests, more cache invalidations etc.)\n\nI\u0027m fine with options 1. and 2., but I prefer to chose the same approach for updating verifiers and for updating verification details.",
      "parentUuid": "0f629997_d249a539",
      "revId": "41c135497c159f450c159ab3f7902eda817c3c0e",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "41c800dc_0c718c28",
        "filename": "Documentation/rest-api-verifiers.txt",
        "patchSetId": 2
